<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of The Hague (1720)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1720">1720</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-quadruple-alliance">War of the Quadruple Alliance</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of The Hague (1720) was signed on 17 February 1720, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-quadruple-alliance">War of the Quadruple Alliance</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-spain">Philip V of Spain</a> gave up his conquests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-amadeus-ii">Victor Amadeus II</a> of Savoy exchanged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> with Emperor Charles VI. The exchange created the Kingdom of Sardinia under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, the state that later led the unification of Italy.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> of 1713, which ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>, rested on the principle that neither <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> nor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> could be united with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Philip V, grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, was confirmed as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> king of Spain, but he ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-netherlands">Southern Netherlands</a> to Austria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> to Savoy, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a>. Philip saw the recovery of these lands as vital to his prestige, and it became the main aim of his chief minister, Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giulio-alberoni">Giulio Alberoni</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisabeth-farnese">Elisabeth Farnese</a>, niece of the Duke of Parma, whom Philip married in 1714, supported the policy because she wanted an Italian inheritance for her own children. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> fighting the Austro-Turkish War of 1716 to 1718, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> unopposed in 1717 and landed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in July 1718. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> answered with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1718">Treaty of London</a> on 18 July, which Austria joined on 2 August, and the British fleet under Admiral Byng destroyed the Spanish fleet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cape-passaro">Battle of Cape Passaro</a> on 11 August.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Philip still hoped to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> by negotiation, counting on divisions within the alliance, but in December 1718 a Spanish-backed conspiracy to replace the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> as Regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was uncovered, and France declared war on 2 January 1719. Without control of the sea <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> could not win. An attempt to divert British forces through the Jacobite Rising of 1719 failed, and the Capture of Vigo in October showed how exposed the Spanish coast was to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>. Philip dismissed Alberoni in December 1719 and agreed to end the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>There was little negotiation: by joining the alliance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> accepted the terms set in 1718, and four-year-old Charles of Spain, Philip's third son, was recognised as heir to Parma, Piacenza and Tuscany. The settlement did not end attempts to alter the Utrecht arrangements. In the Treaty of Madrid of 1721 George I agreed to raise the return of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> in Parliament at a favourable opportunity, which the Spanish took as a promise, and commercial tensions led to a new Anglo-Spanish War in 1727.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of The Hague (1720)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
